    <p>
      A miniature pressure sensor is not simply a standard transducer shrunk down. It is a different class of component, engineered to disappear inside a product — a ventilator, a pneumatic valve island, a robotic gripper, a handheld calibrator — where every millimetre of diameter and every milliwatt of power consumption is budgeted in advance. Selection for these embedded and OEM applications follows a different logic from buying a field-mounted transmitter: the sensor has to match not just a pressure range, but a board layout, a supply rail, an assembly process and a volume price point. This guide walks through the decisions that matter for miniature pressure sensors — size class, pressure range, output signal, mounting, accuracy and OEM procurement — with the practical rules that get an embedded design to production without a re-spin.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-is">What Is a Miniature Pressure Sensor?</h2>
    <p>
      In the pressure instrumentation industry, "miniature" is a practical category rather than a formal standard. As a rule of thumb, a miniature pressure sensor fits within a roughly <strong>12-19 mm diameter envelope</strong> and a body length of 20-40 mm, with a sensing element built on a stainless steel or ceramic diaphragm between 6 and 15 mm across. Standard industrial transducers, by contrast, typically run 27-30 mm in diameter with large process housings, DIN connectors and display options. The miniature class trades that bulk for weight, footprint and cost — a typical unit weighs tens of grams rather than hundreds.
    </p>
    <p>
      Inside the small package, the core is the same technology family used in larger instruments: a <strong>strain-gauge bridge</strong> bonded to a diaphragm, or a piezoresistive silicon die mounted on a steel or ceramic substrate. What changes is everything around the sensing element — the electronics, the housing, the electrical termination and the calibration — all of which must be miniaturized alongside it. Many miniature sensors ship as a <strong>bare module or PCB-mountable package</strong> rather than a threaded instrument, because that is how embedded designers integrate them.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is worth being precise about terminology before comparing products. A "miniature pressure sensor" in this guide means a small-form-factor pressure-sensing component intended to be integrated into another product, usually in OEM volumes. That is distinct from a compact industrial transmitter that happens to have a small body but still terminates in a DIN connector and is mounted on a pipe. Both exist; the selection criteria below apply to the embedded component class. For the broader instrument-level selection process — range, accuracy, output, connections and hazardous-area ratings — see our <a href="/blog/pressure-transducer-selection-guide/">complete pressure transducer selection guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="applications">Typical Applications: Medical, Pneumatics, Robotics & More</h2>
    <p>
      Miniature pressure sensors earn their place wherever space, weight and power budgets are tight and the sensor is one component among dozens. The most common application families:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Medical devices</strong> — ventilators, anesthesia machines, patient monitors and infusion pumps measure airway pressure, breathing-circuit pressure and occlusion pressure. Small size and low volume are non-negotiable, and biocompatibility of wetted materials matters in patient-connected paths.</li>
      <li><strong>Pneumatic systems</strong> — valve islands, pressure regulators, air preparation units and pneumatic actuators need compact sensors that mount close to the valve or manifold. Ranges are low, typically 0-1 bar to 0-10 bar, and response time matters for closed-loop control.</li>
      <li><strong>Robotics and automation</strong> — collaborative grippers, end-of-arm tooling and force-controlled actuators sense air pressure to estimate grip force and detect part presence. Here the sensor often runs on a 3.3 V or 5 V logic rail inside a crowded controller board.</li>
      <li><strong>Handheld instruments</strong> — portable pressure calibrators, digital manometers, leak testers and field test kits demand sensors small and light enough to fit a handheld enclosure while keeping battery life acceptable.</li>
      <li><strong>Test fixtures and leak testing</strong> — production leak-test machines, filter test stands and function test rigs embed multiple miniature sensors to monitor several test ports simultaneously; low dead volume and fast settling shorten test cycles.</li>
      <li><strong>HVAC and white goods</strong> — compact sensors monitor filter blockage, airflow and refrigerant pressure in air handling units, heat pumps and appliances where the board space allocated to sensing is a few square centimetres.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A useful way to think about these applications is by integration depth. Medical and handheld devices usually integrate the sensor as a component on a PCB, sometimes with a hose barb for media connection. Pneumatic and robotics applications often need a small threaded or manifold-mounted sensor that can be serviced. Test fixtures tend to buy in moderate volumes with a fixed, repeatable specification. Each pattern points to a different mounting style and output format, covered next.
    </p>

    <h2 id="key-specs">Key Specifications: Size, Range, Output & Supply</h2>
    <p>
      Five specifications dominate miniature sensor selection, and they interact in ways that catch first-time integrators out:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Specification</th>
            <th>Typical miniature range</th>
            <th>Selection note</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Body diameter</td>
            <td>12-19 mm (module or threaded)</td>
            <td>12 mm class fits most embedded envelopes; larger diameters allow more electronics and better temperature compensation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pressure range</td>
            <td>0-1 bar to 0-100 bar (gauge)</td>
            <td>Most embedded applications sit at the low end; higher ranges need thicker diaphragms and lose sensitivity</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Output</td>
            <td>mV bridge, 0.5-4.5 V ratiometric, I2C/SPI digital</td>
            <td>mV needs external amplification; digital needs a host microcontroller; amplified is the plug-and-play middle</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Supply voltage</td>
            <td>3.3 V or 5 V DC (amplified/digital)</td>
            <td>Ratiometric outputs track the supply, which suits battery and unregulated rail designs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Accuracy</td>
            <td>±0.25% FS to ±1% FS</td>
            <td>Physical size limits compensation hardware; read the temperature coefficients as carefully as the headline number</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The range decision follows the same rule as industrial transducers — <strong>operate in the middle third of the span and keep overload headroom</strong> — but with an extra constraint: in a miniature package the diaphragm is smaller and thinner, so overpressure capability is proportionally tighter. If your system sees regular pressure spikes, state the worst-case transient rather than the normal working pressure when requesting a quotation. Most miniature sensors tolerate roughly 150-200% of full scale as a burst limit, but that figure must be confirmed on the datasheet rather than assumed.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a comparison against the instrument-level class, the practical differences are shown below.
    </p>
