    <h2 id="mounting">Mounting Options: PCB, Thread, Bonding & Hose Barb</h2>
    <p>
      How the sensor attaches to your product is a mechanical decision that is surprisingly easy to get wrong, because it couples the electrical design to the media path. Four mounting families cover the majority of miniature applications:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>PCB mount</strong> — the sensor sits directly on the board with solder pins or a short flex lead, and pressure is delivered through a port or hose barb on top. This is the cheapest and most compact option, ideal for medical, handheld and HVAC electronics. Plan the board layout around the port position early — re-routing a board to accommodate a port is an expensive discovery.</li>
      <li><strong>Threaded miniature port</strong> — small threads such as M5, M10 or 1/8" NPT allow the sensor to screw into a manifold, valve body or test fixture. This suits pneumatic and hydraulic manifolds where the sensor must be replaceable in the field and sealed with an O-ring or sealing washer.</li>
      <li><strong>Bonding / glue-in</strong> — a portless sensor module is bonded into a recess in the customer's housing with an adhesive, with the media sealed by an O-ring around the diaphragm area. This maximizes design freedom for the OEM but requires qualified adhesive application and a controlled assembly process.</li>
      <li><strong>Hose barb / tube</strong> — a barbed port accepts a push-on silicone or PU tube, common in medical breathing circuits and low-pressure pneumatics up to a few bar. Tube size (typically 1.5-4 mm ID) must be matched to the barb; overtightening clamps can distort thin-walled miniature housings.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Three practical cautions apply across all four options. First, <strong>keep the media path short</strong> — long internal passages add dead volume, slow the response and trap air bubbles or contamination. Second, <strong>protect the diaphragm during assembly</strong>: miniature diaphragms are easily damaged by tooling, solder flux or debris, so specify a protective cap or handling instruction in the assembly line. Third, <strong>decide the sealing method with the supplier</strong>; for threaded and bonded mounts the sealing detail (O-ring material, torque, adhesive type) is usually specified jointly so the sensor warranty and the enclosure sealing remain coherent.
    </p>

    <h2 id="output-signal">Output Signal Selection: mV Bridge vs Amplified vs Digital</h2>
    <p>
      The output format is the single decision that most shapes the rest of the electrical design, because it determines whether your board needs an amplifier, an ADC channel, or a digital bus. The three mainstream miniature options:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Raw mV bridge</strong> — the strain-gauge bridge output of roughly 1-3 mV/V. At 5 V supply that is a 5-15 mV full-scale signal. It is the cheapest and most thermally transparent option, but the host board must provide a precision amplifier and ADC, plus the bridge excitation. Choose this when you already have an analog front end or need maximum control over signal conditioning.</li>
      <li><strong>Amplified 0.5-4.5 V (ratiometric)</strong> — the sensor contains its own conditioning electronics and outputs a voltage proportional to pressure, ratiometric to the supply. It is the most common embedded choice: one wire for supply, one for signal, no external amplifier needed, and the ratiometric behavior cancels supply variations in battery designs. The trade-off is a small PCB-area and cost premium inside the sensor, and output noise that is now fixed by the sensor's internal electronics.</li>
      <li><strong>Digital I2C / SPI</strong> — the sensor outputs calibrated pressure values directly on a digital bus, with temperature readings included. This suits microcontrollers with spare bus capacity, removes all analog noise concerns, and enables per-device calibration data to ride along with the sensor. The costs are a slightly larger package, a firmware dependency, and bus address planning when several sensors share one line.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Aspect</th>
            <th>mV bridge</th>
            <th>0.5-4.5 V ratiometric</th>
            <th>I2C / SPI digital</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Full-scale signal</td>
            <td>~1-3 mV/V (5-15 mV at 5 V)</td>
            <td>0.5-4.5 V</td>
            <td>Calibrated engineering units</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Host electronics needed</td>
            <td>Amplifier + ADC + excitation</td>
            <td>ADC input only</td>
            <td>I2C/SPI port + driver</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Supply sensitivity</td>
            <td>Direct (needs stable excitation)</td>
            <td>Ratiometric — cancels supply drift</td>
            <td>Regulated internally</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Temperature data</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>Yes, on-bus</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Noise immunity</td>
            <td>Low — keep leads short</td>
            <td>Moderate</td>
            <td>High</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Best for</td>
            <td>Custom analog front ends, cost-critical</td>
            <td>Most embedded designs, battery power</td>
            <td>Multi-sensor boards, MCU-based products</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When in doubt, the 0.5-4.5 V ratiometric output is the safe default for a first embedded design: it needs nothing more than an ADC channel, works across 3.3 V and 5 V supplies, and keeps the analog design inside the sensor where the supplier controls it. Move to digital output when you need temperature compensation data, multiple sensors on one bus, or maximum noise immunity in a motor-driven product. Raw mV output is the specialist choice for teams that already own a precision analog front end.
    </p>

    <h2 id="accuracy-temp">Accuracy & Temperature Drift in Miniature Packages</h2>
    <p>
      Accuracy in miniature sensors is quoted the same way as in larger instruments — a percentage of full scale, typically <strong>±0.25% FS to ±1% FS</strong> — but the physical size has a real consequence: there is less room for compensation hardware, so temperature performance deserves extra scrutiny. A sensor that is ±0.5% FS at 25 °C can drift noticeably over a 0-50 °C ambient swing if the temperature coefficients are loose, and that drift is often larger than the headline accuracy figure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check three datasheet values rather than one:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Zero temperature coefficient</strong> — how much the zero reading shifts per 10 °C. This dominates the error budget in applications where the sensor idles at zero pressure (leak testing, monitoring) between cycles.</li>
      <li><strong>Span temperature coefficient</strong> — how much the full-scale output shifts per 10 °C. This dominates in applications measuring a relatively steady pressure where accuracy across temperature matters continuously.</li>
      <li><strong>Long-term stability</strong> — drift of the zero and span over a year. Miniature sensors used in calibrated instruments (handheld calibrators, test stands) need this stated explicitly so the calibration interval can be set.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Digital-output sensors mitigate temperature effects in a different way: the silicon die carries an on-board temperature sensor, and the calibration coefficients applied digitally can compensate the temperature curve at many points rather than with a single analog trim. For wide-temperature embedded designs this is usually the more accurate path, at the cost of a small firmware dependency. Amplified analog sensors use analog compensation, which is simpler to integrate but compensates over fewer calibration points.
    </p>
    <p>
      One further trade-off deserves attention: <strong>sensitivity vs overload</strong>. Within the same 12 mm class, a sensor rated 0-1 bar has a thin, sensitive diaphragm and limited overpressure margin, while a 0-100 bar unit is mechanically robust but far less sensitive at low pressures. Trying to cover both with one part means compromising both. If your product family spans low- and high-pressure variants, plan two part numbers from the start rather than forcing one range to do double duty.
    </p>

    <h2 id="oem-volume">Volume & OEM Procurement: Customization, Calibration & Consistency</h2>
    <p>
      Miniature sensors are overwhelmingly purchased in volume, and the procurement model is different from buying an off-the-shelf transducer from a distributor. OEM programs typically start with a specification review, proceed through samples and qualification, and only then move to volume supply. The things that matter at each stage:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Custom calibration ranges</strong> — a manufacturer can calibrate the sensor to your exact range (for example 0-6 bar rather than a catalogue 0-10 bar) and to your required accuracy, so the sensor's full output span maps onto your application's real operating window. State the working range, the required accuracy and the temperature envelope together.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom terminations and packaging</strong> — PCB pin layout, flex lead length, connector type, hose barb size and even the labelling on the housing can be adapted for your assembly line. These small customizations are where most of the "OEM value" of a volume program lives.</li>
      <li><strong>Electrical and mechanical options</strong> — supply voltage, output format, pressure port style and sealing method are all adjustable in volume. A datasheet variant is usually available before a full custom part number is justified.</li>
      <li><strong>Lot-to-lot consistency</strong> — for production, the critical metric is not the performance of one sample but the distribution across thousands. Ask for the consistency data and the calibration procedure; a supplier with automated calibration produces tighter distributions than one calibrating by hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Lead time and safety stock</strong> — miniature sensors in custom ranges are typically built to order, so agree the volume forecast, the reorder lead time and the agreed safety stock early. A mid-production surprise is expensive.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A realistic OEM timeline looks like this: specification and quotation in week one, samples with datasheets within two to four weeks, qualification testing by the customer over the following weeks, then a pilot batch before volume ramp. The manufacturer's application engineers should be available during qualification — that is the moment when mounting, sealing and EMC details get resolved against the real product.
    </p>
