    </p>

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <h3>What counts as a "miniature" pressure sensor?</h3>
    <p>
      As a working definition, a miniature pressure sensor fits a roughly 12-19 mm diameter envelope with a body length of 20-40 mm, is supplied as a board-level component or small threaded unit, and is designed to be integrated into another product rather than mounted on a pipe. Weight is typically tens of grams.
    </p>
    <h3>What pressure ranges are available in miniature sensors?</h3>
    <p>
      Gauge ranges from 0-1 bar up to about 0-100 bar cover the large majority of embedded applications — medical breathing circuits, pneumatics, robotics and HVAC. Higher and lower ranges exist but become increasingly specialized; within the same package, low ranges trade away overload margin and high ranges trade away sensitivity.
    </p>
    <h3>Should I choose a mV output or an amplified output?</h3>
    <p>
      Choose the amplified 0.5-4.5 V ratiometric output unless your design already has a precision analog front end. The mV bridge signal (about 1-3 mV/V) requires a quality amplifier, ADC and stable excitation on your board; the amplified output needs only an ADC input and is the most reliable choice for a first embedded design.
    </p>
    <h3>When should I use a digital I2C or SPI output sensor?</h3>
    <p>
      Choose digital output when you need on-board temperature compensation data, when several sensors share one bus, or when noise immunity matters inside a motor-driven product. It also removes analog scaling and calibration from your firmware. The trade-offs are a slightly larger package and a firmware dependency on the sensor driver.
    </p>
    <h3>Can the sensor be calibrated to my exact pressure range?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes — OEM programs routinely calibrate to a customer's exact range, accuracy and temperature envelope. The usual path is to start from a standard part number, confirm the custom calibration specification in quotation, then qualify samples before volume supply. This is standard practice for volume programs rather than a special service.
    </p>
    <h3>How much overpressure can a miniature sensor withstand?</h3>
    <p>
      Typical burst limits are around 150-200% of full scale, but the figure depends on the range, the diaphragm construction and the package. Always confirm the overpressure and burst ratings on the datasheet, and state your worst-case transient pressure in the specification so the supplier can confirm the margin.
    </p>

    <h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
    <p>
      Selecting a miniature pressure sensor is an embedded design decision, not an instrumentation purchase. Fix the size class and pressure range first, then choose the output format that matches your board's electronics — amplified ratiometric as the safe default, digital when you need temperature data or bus integration, raw mV only when you already own an analog front end. Verify mounting and sealing with the mechanical design, read the temperature coefficients rather than only the headline accuracy, and plan the OEM program around custom calibration, sampling and lot-to-lot consistency. Worked through in that order, the choice collapses to a small set of sensible candidates — and the remaining details are exactly what a manufacturer's application engineer resolves in the sample round.
    </p>
